= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <matrix proportional to its transpose>, applying the <transpose> twice gives $A^T=5A$ and therefore $A=25A$. Over the <real numbers> or <complex numbers>, $24A=0$ implies
$$
\boxed{A=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $P=I-B$, $Q=I+B$, so $P^T=Q$ and $Q^T=P$. The factors commute because both are <polynomials> in $B$. Also $P$ has a <matrix inverse> since it is the <transpose> of the invertible $Q$. Thus the <Cayley transform of a real skew-symmetric matrix>, with its parameter replaced by $-B$, satisfies
$$
C^TC=P^{-1}QQ^{-1}P=I.
$$
Hence $C$ is an <orthogonal matrix>. Its inverse is $Q P^{-1}$; this lets us distinguish the <symmetric and skew-symmetric Cayley transforms> below.

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <symmetric matrix> that is also an <orthogonal matrix> satisfies $C=C^{-1}$. Multiplying by the commuting invertible factors gives $(I-B)^2=(I+B)^2$, hence $4B=0$. Thus **there is no nonzero choice of $B$**. The only symmetric case is $B=0$, giving $C=I$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Yes, in even dimension.** For example, choose the <skew-symmetric matrix>
$$
\boxed{B=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad C=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}=-B.}
$$
Here $B^2=-I$, so $(I+B)^{-1}=(I-B)/2$ and $C=(I-B)^2/2=-B$. It is an <orthogonal skew-symmetric matrix>. More generally the skew-symmetry condition $C=-C^{-1}$ gives $(I-B)^2=-(I+B)^2$, or $B^2=-I$; no real odd-dimensional matrix can satisfy that condition.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $S_m=I+D+\cdots+D^m$. The finite <matrix geometric series> telescopes to $(I-D)S_m=I-D^{m+1}$. By the assumed convergence, its terms $D^{m+1}$ tend to zero, so the <limit> $S$ satisfies $(I-D)S=I$. Consequently $S=(I-D)^{-1}$. This is the finite-dimensional <Neumann series> argument; invertibility by itself would not imply convergence.

For the stated <nilpotent matrix>, $D^3=0$ and only the first three terms remain:
$$
\boxed{(I-D)^{-1}=I+D+D^2=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\0&1&1\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <characteristic polynomial> is $\det(\lambda I-M)=\lambda(\lambda^2-2)$. Solving the three <eigenvector> equations gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\sqrt2,\ 0,\ -\sqrt2;\qquad v_+=(1,\sqrt2,1)^T,\quad v_0=(1,0,-1)^T,\quad v_-=(1,-\sqrt2,1)^T.}
$$
Each <eigenspace> consists of the <scalar multiples> of its displayed nonzero <vector>. Their pairwise <dot products> vanish: $v_+\cdot v_-=1-2+1=0$ and $v_\pm\cdot v_0=1-1=0$. This also agrees with the <orthogonal diagonalization of a real symmetric matrix>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Euclidean norms> are $\|v_\pm\|=2$ and $\|v_0\|=\sqrt2$. With normalized <eigenvectors> as rows,
$$
\boxed{R=\begin{pmatrix}1/2&1/\sqrt2&1/2\\1/\sqrt2&0&-1/\sqrt2\\1/2&-1/\sqrt2&1/2\end{pmatrix},\qquad RMR^T=\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt2,0,-\sqrt2).}
$$
The rows form an <orthonormal basis>, so $RR^T=I$ and $R$ is an <orthogonal matrix>. The zero <eigenvalue> means $Mv_0=0$ for a nonzero <vector>; thus **$M$ is singular** and has no <matrix inverse>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The upper-left block is a planar <rotation matrix>. Its real <characteristic polynomial> is $(\lambda-\cos\theta)^2+\sin^2\theta$, which is strictly positive for real $\lambda$ because the angle excludes integer multiples of $\pi$. Hence any real <eigenvector> has $x=y=0$. The remaining equation is $5z=\lambda z$, giving
$$
\boxed{\lambda=5,\qquad (x,y,z)=(0,0,z),\quad z\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}.}
$$
There are infinitely many nonzero solutions, spanning **one independent eigenvector direction**.

