= Solution
{parent=i}

Since $(1-i)^2=-2i$, division in the <complex numbers> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{2+i}{(1-i)^2}=\frac{i(2+i)}2=-\frac12+i.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiplying by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator gives $(1+i)/(i-1)=-i$. Cubing yields
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{i+1}{i-1}\right)^3=i.}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $-8i=8e^{-i\pi/2}$. The three distinct <complex roots> are $z=2e^{i(-\pi/6+2k\pi/3)}$, $k=0,1,2$. In Cartesian form,
$$
\boxed{z=\sqrt3-i,\quad 2i,\quad-\sqrt3-i.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The right-hand side has <modulus> $4$ and <argument> $\pi/3$. Its <square roots> have modulus $2$ and arguments $\pi/6$ and $7\pi/6$, giving
$$
\boxed{z=\sqrt3+i\quad\text{or}\quad z=-\sqrt3-i.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

<Complex exponentiation> uses a branch of the <complex logarithm>. All logarithms of $i$ are $i(\pi/2+2\pi k)$ with $k\in\mathbb Z$, so
$$
i^{1-i}=\exp\bigl[(1+i)(\pi/2+2\pi k)\bigr].
$$
Since the trigonometric factor is always $i$, all values in Cartesian form are
$$
\boxed{z=0+i\,e^{\pi/2+2\pi k},\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The <principal logarithm> selects $k=0$ and gives the principal value $ie^{\pi/2}$.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the <complex exponential> forms of the <sine> and <cosine>, and put $w=e^{2iz}$. Multiplying the equation by $2e^{iz}$ gives $(2-i)(w-1)/i+(w+1)=0$, which simplifies to $w=1-i$. Taking all branches of the <complex logarithm>,
$$
2iz=\frac12\ln2+i\left(-\frac\pi4+2\pi n\right).
$$
Thus every solution is
$$
\boxed{z=n\pi-\frac\pi8-\frac{i}{4}\ln2,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The exponential factor used to multiply the equation is nonzero, so no solutions were introduced or lost.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a sufficiently smooth <vector field> on an oriented piecewise-smooth <surface> $S$ with boundary $\partial S$, <Stokes theorem> states
$$
\oint_{\partial S}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\iint_S(\nabla\times\mathbf F)\cdot\mathbf n\,dS.
$$
Here $\mathbf n$ is the chosen <unit normal>, $dS$ is the scalar surface-area element, and $d\mathbf r$ follows the positively oriented boundary. The <boundary orientation> is fixed by the right-hand rule: the fingers curl along $\partial S$ when the thumb points along $\mathbf n$.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The Cartesian <curl> is obtained by cross-differentiating the components:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F=(\partial_yF_z-\partial_zF_y,\ \partial_zF_x-\partial_xF_z,\ \partial_xF_y-\partial_yF_x)=(-2yz,4xz^2,0).}
$$

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**No scalar potential exists on the full three-dimensional domain.** A sufficiently smooth <gradient field> has zero <curl>, since mixed <partial derivatives> commute. The calculated <curl> is not identically zero. The fact that it vanishes at some individual points does not make the whole field a <gradient field>.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Orient the triangle by the path $A\to B\to C\to A$. The <cross product> $(B-A)\times(C-A)=(0,1,0)$ gives the positive <normal vector>. In the plane $y=1$, the triangular region is $x,z\geq0$, $x+z\leq1$. The surface integral in <Stokes theorem> is therefore
$$
\int_0^1\int_0^{1-z}4xz^2\,dx\,dz=2\int_0^1z^2(1-z)^2\,dz=\frac1{15}.
$$
For the boundary <line integral>, $AB$ has $z=0$, so contributes zero. On $BC$, $x=0$ and only $dz$ is nonzero, so its contribution is also zero. Parametrize $CA$ by $(x,y,z)=(t,1,1-t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$. Its contribution is
$$
\int_0^1[2t(1-t)^3-t^2(1-t)^2]dt=\frac1{15}.
$$
Thus **both oriented integrals equal $1/15$**. Reversing the orientation would reverse both signs.

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The coordinate intercepts suggest the <normal vector>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf n=(1/a,1/b,1/c).}
$$
Indeed $\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf a=\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf b=\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf c=1$, so the two independent edge <vectors> in the plane are both <orthogonal> to $\mathbf n$.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <plane equation> in normal form is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf r=1,\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad \frac xa+\frac yb+\frac zc=1.}
$$
An equivalent vector parametrization is $\mathbf r=\mathbf a+s(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)+t(\mathbf c-\mathbf a)$, with $s,t\in\mathbb R$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <distance from a point to a plane>, applied at the origin, is the plane constant divided by the <Euclidean norm> of its <normal vector>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|OD|=\frac1{\sqrt{a^{-2}+b^{-2}+c^{-2}}}.}
$$
The <closest point on a plane> lies on the normal through the origin; this is an <orthogonal projection onto an intercept plane>.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Taking the <dot product> with $\mathbf n$ gives $\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf r=\lambda+\mu+\nu$. The necessary and sufficient condition is therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda+\mu+\nu=1.}
$$
These coefficients describe an <affine combination> of the three vertices, or their <barycentric coordinate system>. Nonnegativity would additionally restrict the point to the triangle; it is not required for the whole plane.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Put $S=a^{-2}+b^{-2}+c^{-2}$. The <orthogonal projection onto an intercept plane> is $\mathbf d=\mathbf n/S$. Comparing its coordinates with $(\lambda a,\mu b,\nu c)$ gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac{a^{-2}}S,\qquad\mu=\frac{b^{-2}}S,\qquad\nu=\frac{c^{-2}}S.}
$$
Their sum is one, as the <plane equation> requires.

= f
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The <vector> $\mathbf a$ is <orthogonal> to both $\mathbf b$ and $\mathbf c$, so $\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf c)=0$. Also $\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf b=\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf c=1/S$, hence $\mathbf d\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf c)=0$. Subtraction gives $(\mathbf a-\mathbf d)\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf c)=0$. Both vectors are nonzero, so
$$
\boxed{\text{the angle is }\pi/2.}
$$
This is the altitude property of the <orthogonal projection onto an intercept plane>: the projection point is the triangle's <orthocenter>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <scalar triple product> is a <determinant>:
$$
(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\cdot\mathbf c=\det(\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c).
$$
A cyclic permutation of its three columns is an <even permutation>, so it leaves the determinant unchanged. Thus $\det(\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c)=\det(\mathbf b,\mathbf c,\mathbf a)=\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)$, proving the identity.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <vector triple product identity> twice:
$$
(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\times\mathbf c=\mathbf b(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)-\mathbf a(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c),\qquad \mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)=\mathbf b(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)-\mathbf c(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b).
$$
Subtracting cancels the common term, yielding
$$
\boxed{(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\times\mathbf c-\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)=\mathbf c(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)-\mathbf a(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c).}
$$
It is generally nonzero: the <cross product> does not satisfy the <associative property>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

With $s=\sin\theta_0$ and $c=\cos\theta_0$, conversion from <spherical coordinates> gives $\mathbf a=r_0(s,0,c)$, $\mathbf b=r_0(-s/2,\sqrt3s/2,c)$ and $\mathbf c=r_0(-s/2,-\sqrt3s/2,c)$. The <volume> of the <parallelepiped> is the <absolute value> of their <scalar triple product>:
$$
\boxed{V=|\det(\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c)|=\frac{3\sqrt3}{2}r_0^3\sin^2\theta_0\,|\cos\theta_0|.}
$$
The absolute value matters when the polar angle is in the lower hemisphere. At the poles or equator the three edge vectors are linearly dependent and the volume vanishes.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $G$ denote carrying the <gene> and $+$ a positive result. The <law of total probability> gives
$$
\boxed{P(+)=P(+\mid G)P(G)+P(+\mid G^c)P(G^c)=0.99(0.001)+0.05(0.999)=0.05094\simeq0.0509.}
$$
The last value is to three significant figures. The much larger non-carrier population contributes most of the positive tests in this mathematical model.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By <Bayes' theorem>, the required <conditional probability> is
$$
\boxed{P(G\mid+)=\frac{P(+\mid G)P(G)}{P(+)}=\frac{0.00099}{0.05094}=0.0194\quad\text{(3 s.f.)}.}
$$
This is about $1.94\%$; it is a different conditioning direction from the test's $99\%$ positive probability among carriers.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The negative-test <probability> is $P(-)=1-0.05094=0.94906$. Applying <Bayes' theorem> again,
$$
\boxed{P(G\mid-)=\frac{P(-\mid G)P(G)}{P(-)}=\frac{0.01(0.001)}{0.94906}=1.05\times10^{-5}\quad\text{(3 s.f.)}.}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $B$ to make a first throw, $A$ must first pass, with <probability> $1/2$. For $B$ then to win on that throw, $B$ must roll one of two winning faces, with probability $1/3$. The throws are <independent>, giving
$$
\boxed{P=\frac12\frac13=\frac16.}
$$
A first throw of $3$ by $A$ would make $B$ win without throwing, and is excluded by the requested event.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Before $A$'s second throw, $A$ can win either immediately, with <probability> $1/3$, or by passing to $B$ and having $B$ throw $3$, with probability $(1/2)(1/6)$. These are disjoint events, so
$$
\boxed{P=\frac13+\frac1{12}=\frac5{12}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

This is a <two-turn renewal game>. A full pair of throws has probability $(1/2)^2=1/4$ of passing twice and returning to the original state. The probability that $A$ wins before that restart is $5/12$. Thus <first-step analysis> gives $p=5/12+p/4$, or
$$
\boxed{P(A\text{ wins})=\frac59.}
$$
Equivalently sum the <geometric series> $(5/12)\sum_{j\geq0}(1/4)^j$. The probability of surviving infinitely many pairs is zero, so the eventual winner is well defined with probability one.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the <Lagrange multiplier> equations, use $\mathcal L=x^2y+2y-\lambda(x^2+y^2-1)$. They give $2x(y-\lambda)=0$, $x^2+2=2\lambda y$, and $x^2+y^2=1$. If $x\ne0$, then $\lambda=y$, implying $x^2+2=2y^2$ and therefore $y^2=1$, contradicting $x\ne0$. Thus only $x=0$, $y=\pm1$ remain.

The circle is <compact> and the function is <continuous>, so global extrema exist. On it, eliminating $x^2$ gives $f=3y-y^3$, which is increasing for $-1<y<1$ since its <derivative> is $3(1-y^2)>0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f_{\max}=2\text{ at }(0,1),\qquad f_{\min}=-2\text{ at }(0,-1).}
$$
These constrained extrema are flat to quadratic order along the circle, so a vanishing second derivative alone would not classify them.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Minimize $\frac12\|\mathbf r\|^2$ subject to $\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf r=1$, $\mathbf n_2\cdot\mathbf r=2$, where $\mathbf n_1=(2,1,-1)$ and $\mathbf n_2=(1,-1,1)$. The <Lagrange multiplier> equation gives $\mathbf r=\lambda\mathbf n_1+\mu\mathbf n_2$. Since $\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf n_2=0$, $\|\mathbf n_1\|^2=6$, and $\|\mathbf n_2\|^2=3$, the constraints imply $\lambda=1/6$, $\mu=2/3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r_*=(1,-1/2,1/2).}
$$
No explicit parametrization of the line was needed. For any other feasible point, $\mathbf h=\mathbf r-\mathbf r_*$ is <orthogonal> to both normals and therefore to $\mathbf r_*$. The <Pythagorean theorem> gives $\|\mathbf r\|^2=\|\mathbf r_*\|^2+\|\mathbf h\|^2$, proving the unique global minimum. This is the geometric principle behind the <minimum-norm least-squares solution>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Taylor's theorem> expands about $a$ in powers of $h=x-a$. With the usual differentiability assumptions, its finite form with <Lagrange remainder> is
$$
f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k+\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi)}{(n+1)!}(x-a)^{n+1},
$$
where $\xi$ lies between $a$ and $x$. If the remainder tends to zero as $n\to\infty$, this yields the <Taylor series> $f(x)=\sum_{k\geq0}f^{(k)}(a)(x-a)^k/k!$. Smoothness alone does not guarantee that infinite-series equality.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $h=x-1$. The <Taylor series> of the <natural logarithm> gives the first four nonzero terms:
$$
\boxed{\ln x=h-\frac{h^2}{2}+\frac{h^3}{3}-\frac{h^4}{4}+O(h^5).}
$$
The corresponding infinite series converges for $|h|<1$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the <arctangent>, put $h=x-1$. Its <derivative> is $(d/dh)\arctan(1+h)=1/(2+2h+h^2)=\frac12-\frac h2+\frac{h^2}4+O(h^4)$. Integrating and using $\arctan1=\pi/4$ gives
$$
\boxed{\arctan x=\frac\pi4+\frac h2-\frac{h^2}4+\frac{h^3}{12}+O(h^5).}
$$
The constant counts as the first nonzero term. The quartic coefficient vanishes; the next nonzero term is $-h^5/40$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The removable value at zero is one. Expand the denominator as $e^x-1=x(1+x/2+x^2/6+\cdots)$ and determine the reciprocal coefficients by multiplication. The first four nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{\frac{x}{e^x-1}=1-\frac x2+\frac{x^2}{12}-\frac{x^4}{720}+O(x^6).}
$$
This is the <exponential generating function> of the <Bernoulli numbers>, with convention $B_1=-1/2$. The coefficients of $x^3$ and $x^5$ are zero.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <homogeneous solution> is $A\cos2x+B\sin2x$. A trial $C\cos x$ gives the <particular solution> $\cos x/3$. The two initial conditions set $A=-1/3$, $B=0$, giving
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{\cos x-\cos2x}{3}.}
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use $\cos^2x=(1+\cos2x)/2$. The constant forcing has <particular solution> $1/8$, while the $\cos2x$ term satisfies the <resonance condition> and needs the trial $Cx\sin2x$. Since $(d^2/dx^2+4)(x\sin2x)=4\cos2x$, the forced term is $x\sin2x/8$. Adding the <homogeneous solution> and imposing the initial conditions gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{1-\cos2x+x\sin2x}{8}.}
$$

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The differential operator is $(D-1)^2$, where $D=d/dx$. Set $y=e^xv$; the <product rule> reduces the equation to $v''=1+x$. Twice integrating gives $v=x^2/2+x^3/6+Ax+B$. The initial conditions yield $B=A=0$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=e^x\left(\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^3}{6}\right).}
$$
This substitution removes the repeated characteristic root before finding a <particular solution>.

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The function is <even> and continuous, with period $2\pi$. On $0<x<\pi$ it is $\sin2x$, so it rises from zero to one at $\pi/4$, crosses zero at $\pi/2$, reaches minus one at $3\pi/4$, and returns to zero at $\pi$. Reflect this curve about the vertical axis and repeat periodically. The joins at integer multiples of $\pi$ have corners, but no jumps.

\Image[paper-1-even-sine-extension.png]
{title=Even periodic extension of a sine mode and its Fourier approximation}
{description=Original plot over two periods. The dashed approximation uses the first twelve nonzero cosine coefficients; the exact function is continuous with corners at integer multiples of $\pi$.}

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Because the function is <even>, all sine <Fourier coefficients> vanish. Its average is zero, so $a_0=0$. The <Fourier cosine coefficients of a sine mode> follow from the product-to-sum identity:
$$
a_n=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi\sin2x\cos nx\,dx=\begin{cases}\displaystyle\frac8{\pi(4-n^2)},&n\text{ odd},\\0,&n\text{ even}.\end{cases}
$$
For $n=2$, the integral is directly zero rather than an undefined substitution into a denominator. Thus
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac8\pi\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac{\cos((2m+1)x)}{4-(2m+1)^2}.}
$$
The <Fourier series> equals the continuous function at every point, including the corners. Its coefficients decay quadratically, so the series is also <absolutely convergent>.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At $x=0$, the <Fourier series> equals $f(0)=0$. Isolating the first term gives
$$
0=\frac13+\sum_{m=1}^\infty\frac1{4-(2m+1)^2}.
$$
Changing the sign of the remaining denominators proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{m=1}^\infty\frac1{(2m+1)^2-4}=\frac13.}
$$
The <absolute convergence> justifies the evaluation and rearrangement.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <Laplace's equation>, seek a nonzero separated product $f=X(x)Y(y)$. Dividing by $XY$ gives $X''/X=-Y''/Y$, so both sides are a <separation constant>. Choosing it as $-k^2$ gives $X''+k^2X=0$, $Y''-k^2Y=0$, with trigonometric and <hyperbolic functions>, respectively. A zero constant gives linear factors; a positive constant interchanges the two types. <Boundary conditions> determine which modes are allowed, and <linearity> permits their superposition to match the remaining boundary data.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The zero values at $x=0,a$ select $X_n=\sin(n\pi x/a)$, and the zero value at $y=0$ selects $Y_n=\sinh(n\pi y/a)$. Expanding the constant upper-edge value as a <Fourier sine series> gives coefficients $b_n=2F[1-(-1)^n]/(n\pi)$. The resulting <harmonic function in a rectangle with one constant boundary value> is
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=\frac{4F}{\pi}\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac{\sin((2m+1)\pi x/a)}{2m+1}\frac{\sinh((2m+1)\pi y/a)}{\sinh((2m+1)\pi)},\qquad0<x,y<a.}
$$
Each term satisfies <Laplace's equation> and the three zero boundary values. At $y=a$, the series equals $F$ for $0<x<a$. On compact subsets of the interior it and its derivatives converge exponentially, which justifies the termwise equation.

For $F\ne0$, the top-corner boundary prescriptions conflict: a corner cannot be both zero on a vertical edge and $F$ on the upper edge. The bounded interior solution has the specified limits along the open sides; it has no single continuous extension at those two corners. Values assigned only at the isolated corners do not change the interior solution.

\Image[paper-1-square-harmonic-extension.png]
{title=Harmonic extension from one heated edge of a square}
{description=Original normalized solution $f/F$, with the upper open edge fixed at one and the other three open edges at zero. The top corner circles mark incompatible side limits, not prescribed continuous corner temperatures.}
